# Overview

[Enterprise Refactoring & Safety Protocol — Mandatory]

This project is a large-scale production system.
All refactoring, structural changes, and cross-file edits must follow these rules.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
1. Planning Before Coding (MANDATORY)
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
Before writing or editing any code:
- First analyze current architecture.
- Identify exact files and dependencies.
- Summarize the change plan briefly.
- Only then start implementation.

Never directly modify multiple files without a clear plan.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2. Single Responsibility Patch Rule
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
- One task = one logical change.
- Avoid mixing refactoring + feature + formatting.
- Each patch must have a single purpose.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
3. Minimal Diff Principle (CRITICAL)
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
- Always modify the minimum number of files.
- Prefer small diffs over big rewrites.
- Never refactor unrelated code for "cleanliness".

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
4. File Scope Limitation
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
- Start with single-file modification.
- Only expand to multi-file when dependency requires it.
- Explicitly explain why multiple files are necessary.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
5. KR / EN Dual Update Rule
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
- If both KR and EN versions exist:
  → Always update both.
  → Logic & structure must be identical.
  → Only text differs.
- Never update only one side.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
6. Workspace / Permission Safety Rule (CRITICAL)
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
- Any change touching:
  - workspace
  - role
  - permission
  - billing
  - subscription
  - credits
Must:
  - Preserve backward compatibility.
  - Never break existing permission flows.
  - Always default to safer access (deny over allow).

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
7. Execution Safety Protocol
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
- All shell commands must:
  → Wait for explicit user approval.
  → If approval delay > 10 seconds → cancel & restart.
- No parallel or chained commands.
- lint → build → test flow strictly enforced.
- On first error → stop → minimal fix → re-run.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
8. Progressive Refactor Strategy
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
Large refactors must be broken into:
- Step 1: Structure alignment
- Step 2: Data flow correction
- Step 3: Permission gating
- Step 4: UI cleanup

Never perform large refactors in a single pass.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
9. Regression Safety Rule
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
- Any refactor must:
  - Preserve existing behavior.
  - Not change business logic unless explicitly requested.
  - Not break routes, auth, billing, or workspace.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
10. Completion Criteria
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
- Type errors: 0
- Runtime errors: 0
- lint: pass
- build: pass
- test: pass (if exists)
- No silent failure allowed.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
11. Large-Change Safety Lock
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
If modification exceeds:
- 5 files OR
- 300 lines diff

→ STOP and request confirmation before continuing.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
12. Default Bias
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
Always prefer:
- Stability > elegance
- Explicitness > abstraction
- Simplicity > cleverness

This rule overrides any "optimization" behavior.

